WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.680
ai agents are quietly stripping ads and sluggish menus from smart TVs, turning them fast and simple again. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 200-2. ChatGPT Live Slides for group work, Kling's video tool was sound, a deep dive on how a helper on your home Wi-Fi maps a TV, then quick bits. You know that feeling when movie night is ready, and the smart TV still tracks what you watch and pushes apps you never wanted, a growing number of people, tech crunch reports,

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.780
are fighting back by letting simple artificial intelligence agents take the

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.980
controls and clean house. Artificial intelligence or AI is software that can

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.420
handle tasks that used to need a person. An agent is an AI that can take a

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.340
series of steps on its own, instead of only chatting back. Picture the TV as a

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.920
backpack stuffed with flyers, trackers, and extra apps you never packed. These

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.100
agents connect to the TV over the same Wi-Fi network your phone already uses.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.260
Think of that Wi-Fi as a shared hallway in your house, where every gadget can knock on

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.500
every other gadget's door.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:08.720
Once they are in that hallway, the agents explore the TV's menus the way a curious friend

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.000
pokes around a new app.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.620
They can even add tricks like pulling videos straight from the internet without extra apps.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.240
Almost every teen has sat through a laggy TV during a family stream, so this is not some

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:21.460
far away lab story.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.980
It is AI giving regular people more control over devices they already own.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.420
instead of waiting for companies to fix them.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.520
If the trend grows, future TVs might ship

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:32.860
with fewer forced features,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.880
because users now have an easy way to remove them.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.200
You can try a version of this right now.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.280
Put your computer and your TV on the same Wi-Fi,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.460
then ask an AI-like clawed to help reverse engineer

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.700
the TV's controls with one short prompt.

00:01:47.749 --> 00:01:50.009
Reverse engineer here just means figure out

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.269
the hidden buttons by watching what the TV does.

00:01:52.889 --> 00:01:55.489
It is like learning a new game by tapping every menu

00:01:55.489 --> 00:02:01.089
until the secret settings appear. Many people report it takes about 5 minutes, and it works on

00:02:01.089 --> 00:02:06.789
popular brands like Samsung. Owners say the set feels noticeably snappier once tracking and ad

00:02:06.789 --> 00:02:12.589
services are turned off. The same method also lets users install apps from outside the official store,

00:02:13.009 --> 00:02:19.163
something TV makers usually block. Changing the password on a home router already teaches the whole trick,

00:02:19.623 --> 00:02:25.163
even if you have never thought about TVs this way. You open a browser on any phone or laptop that

00:02:25.163 --> 00:02:30.563
is already on that network, and the router talks back, no special cable, everything on the

00:02:30.563 --> 00:02:32.583
same Wi-Fi can speak to everything else.

00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:37.963
An AI agent does that same knock on the door move, except it is not changing a password.

00:02:38.543 --> 00:02:41.543
It quietly walks every menu and setting on the smart TV.

00:02:42.143 --> 00:02:42.743
Step 1.

00:02:42.963 --> 00:02:45.723
It sends a simple request like, show me the settings page.

00:02:46.203 --> 00:02:46.803
Step 2.

00:02:46.983 --> 00:02:49.823
It follows the on-screen buttons the way you would with a remote.

00:02:50.423 --> 00:02:50.983
Step 3.

00:02:50.983 --> 00:02:56.023
Because the agent and the TV share the network, it sees the response instantly and keeps going.

00:02:56.503 --> 00:03:00.123
It hunts until it finds the hidden options that control ads or speed.

00:03:00.723 --> 00:03:05.503
The whole loop feels like a tiny patient helper sitting next to the TV who never gets

00:03:05.503 --> 00:03:06.203
bored clicking.

00:03:06.783 --> 00:03:11.323
Once the agent learns the layout, it can remember the path and run it again whenever you want

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:12.383
the TV cleaned up.

00:03:12.883 --> 00:03:17.663
It uses the everyday connection you already have to reach a device that normally only talks

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:18.623
to its own apps.

00:03:18.623 --> 00:03:23.623
The agent keeps a running list of every screen it visits so it can jump back without starting

00:03:23.623 --> 00:03:23.983
over.

00:03:24.403 --> 00:03:28.943
That list is like a breadcrumb trail through a maze, or a map you draw while walking

00:03:28.943 --> 00:03:30.203
a new Minecraft world.

00:03:30.623 --> 00:03:35.423
It also notices when a menu changes after a button press, and it adjusts the next move.

00:03:35.923 --> 00:03:39.443
That back and forth continues until the agent has mapped the whole system.

00:03:39.963 --> 00:03:44.363
Then it can carry out the exact sequence of clicks needed to remove bloatware, which

00:03:44.363 --> 00:03:46.743
is the extra junk software you never asked for.

00:03:46.743 --> 00:03:51.823
See it as a patient clicker with a map, and the trick stops feeling like a locked box.

00:03:52.877 --> 00:03:58.277
Open AI added live collaboration to chat GPT slides, so several people can work on

00:03:58.277 --> 00:03:59.957
the same presentation at once.

00:04:00.597 --> 00:04:05.417
Digit reports you type or speak your ideas, and chat GPT turns them into slides.

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:11.137
Classmates or teammates can jump in, edit text, move pictures, or leave comments while

00:04:11.137 --> 00:04:12.897
everyone watches the changes happen.

00:04:12.897 --> 00:04:17.937
It is like a group chat that is also the slide show, instead of one person doing all the

00:04:17.937 --> 00:04:19.837
work because sharing files was a pain.

00:04:20.437 --> 00:04:25.817
You can click directly on any slide to change text, update charts, or resize images without

00:04:25.817 --> 00:04:26.677
leaving the chat.

00:04:27.117 --> 00:04:32.497
Chat GPT or its helper agent can turn those feedback comments into actual edits automatically.

00:04:33.077 --> 00:04:39.617
To try it, open chat GPT, type create chat GPT slides followed by your topic, then share

00:04:39.617 --> 00:04:40.597
the link with friends.

00:04:40.597 --> 00:04:46.237
Live group editing needs a pro, business, or enterprise plan, which are the paid chat

00:04:46.237 --> 00:04:47.217
GPT plans.

00:04:47.797 --> 00:04:51.477
The free version still lets you build single slides so you can test the idea.

00:04:52.037 --> 00:04:56.817
The finished deck can be presented inside chat GPT, or exported straight to PowerPoint

00:04:56.817 --> 00:04:58.017
or Google slides.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:05.130
The video tool Kling just released version 4.0, and the decoder says it can now make 30 second

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:06.370
clips with stereo sound.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:11.790
Stereo sound means the audio has a left and a right side, like headphones, not

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:17.010
a flat beat from one speaker. It can also lip sync in multiple languages, which means

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.770
the mouth on screen matches the words you hear. You give it a prompt or a few keyframe

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:27.110
images, and it generates smooth video that matches the audio. Keyframes are still pictures

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:31.770
that mark the important poses, like comic panels that the motion has to pass through.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:37.690
That opens quick experiments for short stories, music videos, or meme clips without expensive

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:38.290
software.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:44.090
The new version accepts up to 10 keyframe images, and it understands prompts up to 8,000

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:44.770
tokens long.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:50.610
Tokens are the little chunks of words the AI reads, like Lego bricks that stack into a sentence.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:56.330
Go to the Kling website, sign up for the free tier, and try typing a cat dancing on a window

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.670
sill with upbeat music.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:02.250
Watch whether the motion stays smooth, and whether the music actually feels attached

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:02.890
to the picture.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:07.670
A new flash version runs lighter if you want faster results on a regular laptop.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.610
Comparisons with other tools show it keeps pace, but does not pull far ahead.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:16.130
The rapid gains in AI video quality may be slowing.

00:06:17.225 --> 00:06:22.005
Tesla is tying employee stock awards to how many Optimus robots come off the factory line.

00:06:22.845 --> 00:06:27.765
Optimus is Tesla's walking robot, shaped like a person, and those extra shares are the

00:06:27.765 --> 00:06:33.125
bonus. The same actuators already appear on the new Roadster, controlling arrow surfaces

00:06:33.125 --> 00:06:38.865
and tank valves. Actuators are the little motors that move a joint, like muscles, and arrow

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:44.570
surfaces are the flaps that change how air flows over the car. Open AI is improving chat

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:50.050
GPT on old programming languages like Coball, a decades-old language still running important

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:55.050
systems. Banks and governments still depend on that kind of code, and the latest models

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.290
are getting better at reading and updating it without breaking anything.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:04.370
The work focuses on the business logic written in Kobol, Fortran, and Visual Basic, meaning

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:09.210
the hidden rules those programs follow, so developers can modernize systems while keeping

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:10.210
what already works.

